from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "1. The Question, the Method and the Four Chapters"),
("p", "Claim: the question of tongues and prophecy is a question about what four chapters of one letter say, and it can be answered from those chapters if they are read in order. The volume asks of 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 what a reader would ask of any passage: who is addressed, what is said, what reason is given, and what is left unsaid."),
("sub", "1.1 The letter and its readers"),
("p", "Paul opens the letter “unto the church of God which is at Corinth, to them that are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints, with all that in every place call upon the name of Jesus Christ our Lord, both theirs and ours” (1 Corinthians 1:2). The readers are the church at Corinth and every place where the name is called upon. The chapters on gifts begin with the words “Now concerning spiritual gifts, brethren, I would not have you ignorant” (1 Corinthians 12:1). They are an answer, and the phrase “now concerning” marks them as one of several answers in the letter."),
("p", "The readers had been Gentiles. Paul writes, “Ye know that ye were Gentiles, carried away unto these dumb idols, even as ye were led” (1 Corinthians 12:2). The chapters therefore say something to a Gentile church about gifts that had appeared among them, and the volume asks what they say about the purpose of those gifts. It asks also what Paul quotes to the Corinthians, and from where."),
("sub", "1.2 What the chapters contain"),
("p", "The three chapters fall into three parts. Chapter 12 names the gifts, says they are given by one Spirit and compares the church to a body of many members. Chapter 13 sets the gifts beside love and says what becomes of them. Chapter 14 returns to tongues and prophecy and gives the rules for their use in the assembly. A thread runs through all three: the gifts are for the profit of the church, and Paul’s concern is order and edification."),
("tbl", "The three chapters in outline.", [
    ("1 Corinthians 12:1, 2", T("1 Cor 12:1, 2"), "The subject is gifts, and the readers are Gentiles who had been led to idols."),
    ("1 Corinthians 12:30", T("1 Cor 12:30"), "The last of a run of questions that expect the answer no."),
    ("1 Corinthians 13:8", T("1 Cor 13:8"), "Three verbs for three gifts, and the sentence says nothing about when."),
    ("1 Corinthians 14:21, 22", T("1 Cor 14:21, 22"), "Paul quotes the law, names the people, and states the purpose of tongues in his own words."),
  ], [0.22, 0.48, 0.30]),
("p", "The table gives the four verses on which the volume turns. The remaining chapters read the whole passage around them."),
("sub", "1.3 The method"),
("p", "The method is the one the series uses. The volume reads a passage as it stands in its own chapter, records who speaks and who is addressed, notes what Paul says the gift is for, and sets side by side the verses that use the same word. It takes its terms from the King James Version and reports the translators’ italics where they bear on the sense. It does not read a later doctrine back into the verse."),
("p", "Two controls govern the method. The first is the audience control: a passage is read for the people it names. Where Paul quotes the prophet he names a people, and the volume follows the quotation to the prophet and reads it there. The second is the program control: the gifts of Corinth stand in the years Acts records, and the volume reads them in that setting. It does not decide from Corinth alone what happened to the gifts after Acts 28, and it does not carry a statement about the Corinthian gifts into a program the text does not name."),
("sub", "1.4 What the volume does not decide"),
("p", "The volume does not decide what Paul means by “that which is perfect” in 1 Corinthians 13:10. Writers who agree that tongues have ceased and writers who hold that they continue have read the phrase in several ways, and the volume reports the verse exactly as it is written. It does not say that prophecy ended at Acts 28 on the strength of a verse that says so, because no such verse exists. It reasons from the sign-program, from 13:8 and from 14:30 to the end of prophecy and says that it is reasoning. And it does not say anything about God’s power to heal or to speak: the ruling governs the programs."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The chapters are an answer to the Corinthians, written to Gentiles who had been led to idols.",
    "Chapter 12 names the gifts, chapter 13 says what becomes of them and chapter 14 gives the purpose of tongues and prophecy.",
    "The method is to read each passage for its audience and its program and to report the KJV’s own marks.",
    "The meaning of “that which is perfect” is left unidentified, and the ruling does not depend on it.",
  ]),
]
